course: dictionary
lesson: 5826
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. «Early knights wore chain mail and later knights wore fitted plate armour.» Knights and armour are the clues, so mail here is armour made of small metal rings, not letters.
explain: In everyday life, mail usually means letters and parcels sent by post. You check the mail, put a card in the mailbox, or get junk mail. To mail something means to send it by post, like mailing a birthday card to Grandma.
explain: Mail is also short for email, the messages you send on a computer or phone. Context tells you which meaning to use each time, so always read the words around it.
explain: Spellcheck is a helper, not an editor. «The dog bark at the mail man» passes a spellcheck, because every word is a real word. Yet bark should be barked, so always read your own writing again.
fact: Mail means letters and parcels sent by post.
fact: To mail something means to send it by post.
fact: Chain mail is armour made of small metal rings, worn by knights.
fact: Mail is short for email.
fact: Spellcheck can miss mistakes when every word is a real word, so read your work again.
exercise: «Early knights wore chain mail and later knights wore fitted plate armour.» What is chain mail? | A net for catching fish | Letters sent by post by knights | A necklace of linked gold | *Armour made of small metal rings
exercise: Mum says, «Please check the mail.» What does she mean? | Wash the mailbox | *Look for the letters and parcels that have arrived | Count the stamps in the drawer | Send a message to a friend on the phone
exercise: In the sentence «Early knights wore chain mail», which word tells you what kind of mail it is? | wore | knights | *chain | early
test: A post office mails 6 parcels each day for 5 days. How many parcels is that? | 56 | 11 | 5 | *30
test: To mail something means: | To open it and read it | To count the stamps on it | *To send it by post | To lose it on the way
exam: Which is a safety rule about mail? | *Never open mail or emails from strangers that ask for passwords | Always give your password to anyone who asks nicely | Throw away every letter without reading it | Post your password on the front of a parcel
